  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Miller Peak</strong> is a 9,468-foot summit in Arizona. The Miller Peak Trail covers 0.8 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 361 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Easy overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. From the trailhead at about 9,052 feet, the route tops out near 9,364 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.3915,-110.29601\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">31.3915, -110.29601</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 70 miles southeast of Tucson, Arizona. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Easy rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 0.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Apache Peak, Mount Wrightson and Mount Hopkins \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
